quick push-up routine that will increase  your pushing power body control and  general functional performance and it  only takes ten minutes this one's quite  unlike most circuits or programs you may  have used before because it consists  entirely of one move the push-up but to  increase the challenge and to keep  things interesting will be varying the  cadence the angles and the types of  contraction use most programs are  entirely focused on the fast repetitions  using submaximal weights you pump out a  few reps and sets then you head on to  the next movement you don't truly master  any one of these movements what happens  we need to be stronger what happens when  you need to exert strength for an  extended period and have you really  mastered the movement if you can't also  perform it slowly this is what I mean  when I say dynamic strength the ability  to be explosive when you need to be  force to be slow and controlled or to  churn out lots of reps to be able to  adapt to the demands of any situation  the aim here therefore is to master the  push-up by targeting every type of  muscle fiber and every facet of strength  in that range of motion it goes like  this overcoming isometric push-ups or  floor hugs for three times six seconds  explosive clapping push-ups so technical  failure one-handed push-ups to failure  push-ups to failure quasi isometric  push-ups for thirty Seconds typically  you'll rest for thirty seconds between  each set and repeat the circuit for a  total three to five rounds the rest  between rounds is also only thirty  seconds the short rest time ensures that  you're going into each set already  fatigued which will force the body to  switch its muscle fiber recruitment in  other words because you've used all of  the slow twitch fiber your body has no  choice but to recruit faster twitch  muscle fiber even for those slower  movements try it and suffer  alternatively you can perform this  pushup routine as a single drop set so  that you move on to the next step  immediately after finishing the last one  in this case you should stop each  exercise slightly before failure in  order to ensure you are still able to  perform this method is less effective  for building the complete push-up  strength but I'll allow you to get a  quick and intense workout that can also  be tacked onto a full body routine or  push day so now we're going to break  down each movement as mentioned the aim  of the routine is to take a single  movement and tackle it in every way this  is how that works so the overcoming  isometric movement is one that involves  pushing or pulling against an immovable  force this is equivalent to trying to  lift a barbell that's too heavy for you  meaning that you can exert  maximum effort and maximum neural drive  in other words the brain tries to  recruit every last muscle fiber it can  which can improve the muscle fiber  recruitment overtime and thus increased  maximum strength in other words you can  tap into more of the strength that's  already there this may also help you to  access more explosive force in the next  set thanks to the post activation  principle normally overcoming isometrics  involve using some kind of external tool  but with this move which i've kind of  invented we're going to use the floor to  create a bodyweight overcoming isometric  to do this you widen your arms so that  they are too wide to push you up then  you're going to try and push up  nonetheless straining against the ground  and not going anywhere it should look  like you're trying to PEC fly or to hug  the floor or as I prefer to crush the  earth you're going to hold each one of  these for six seconds with a maximal  effort then release and rest for 10  seconds and then repeat for the next  part of this push-up routine you'll be  performing explosive push-ups  showboating and clapping optional this  is plyometric training which again  activates as many fast twitch muscle  fibers as possible for a display of  plyometric explosive strength it's all  help you to improve your rate of force  development meaning that you can access  more strength more quickly next we're  moving on to one-handed push-ups  this movement is unilateral so it  isolates a single PEC tricep and  shoulder this allows you to increase the  load such that you are now likely  working with an equivalent of more  weight you're also going to be bracing  your entire body more with your obliques  to combat the rotational force this  means you're using slightly different  intramuscular coordination following the  clapping push-ups in the overcoming  isometrics you may already be facing  some fatigue  [Music]  next we're gonna be doing regular  push-ups for many of us it's relatively  easy to perform huge numbers of push-ups  normally this should now be harder  following what has immediately preceded  this move this is why we're keeping  those rest periods very short  you'll still likely be able to do a lot  of push-ups though when you combine this  with everything you've already done this  means you're now working at work  capacity and your muscle endurance  you're using more slow-twitch muscle  fiber which will help to build more work  capacity it'll increase blood flow to  the area encouraging hypertrophy to  final you're going to end this push-up  routine by performing a quasi isometric  pushup this means you'll be slowing the  movement all the way down taking 15  seconds on the way down and 15 seconds  on the way back up if you're confident  you can increase this to 30 seconds each  way the quasi isometric pushup of course  requires a great amount of slow twitch  muscle fiber as well as a huge amount of  muscle control because there's no  acceleration you won't be able to rely  on momentum to jump past sticking points  in the movement what's more is that  you'll build great for prior ception and  body awareness as you consciously move  for each point make sure you're using  perfect technique so your back is in  that hollow body position and you're  coming all the way down to the ground  this will make the movement much more  efficient in future and this is  especially true if you use a passive  isometric on the way down that means  you're using only the necessary muscle  to go through the movement and not  contracting anything excessive or extra  on top of that now the best part is that  when you start round two and thirty  Seconds the fact that your slow twitch  muscle fiber has been exhausted means  that you'll be forced to rely on a great  proportion of fast twitch muscle fiber  emulating a heavier weight  so many training routines focus on  hitting the same muscle groups with  slightly different exercises here the  exercise is largely the same but what  varies is the speed and the intent that  you train it with you'll find that even  though you're exhausted from maxing out  your regular push-ups you're still  capable of performing slow quasi  isometric reps why because these use the  muscles and the nervous system in  entirely different ways and we can  perform normally explosively  unilaterally and with extreme control  and precision you'll be able to say that  you have mastered that movement and it  makes for one hell of a workout that  creates a lot of pump and that should  trigger hypertrophy no matter what kind  of training you respond bestie and this  carries over into many other aspects of  your performance so enjoy oh and the  same approach used in this push-up  routine can also be used with many  different exercises for example I'm  currently using it to master the pseudo  planche push-up tip with fans for the  useful interesting guys let me know in  the comments if you gave it a try and  what you thought and leave a like if you  did find it useful you can check out my  ebook and training program super  functional training in the link down  below or just subscribe to this channel  for more content like this thanks a ton  for watching and I'll see you next time  bye for now

